### Why do we need partitions in Kafka?

Partitions are the fundamental mechanism that gives Kafka its three core properties: parallelism, scalability, and ordering control. Without partitions, a topic would be a single log on a single broker, and only one consumer could process it at a time. With partitions, the same topic can be spread across many brokers and consumed by many consumers simultaneously.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.AdminClient;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.NewTopic;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class PartitionedTopicCreator {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");

        try (AdminClient admin = AdminClient.create(props)) {
            // 12 partitions = up to 12 parallel consumers
            NewTopic topic = new NewTopic("transactions", 12, (short) 3);
            admin.createTopics(Collections.singleton(topic)).all().get();
            System.out.println("Topic 'transactions' created: 12 partitions");
            System.out.println("Max parallel consumers per group: 12");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Topic 'transactions' created: 12 partitions
Max parallel consumers per group: 12
```

### How do partitions enable parallelism?

A consumer group can have at most as many active consumers as there are partitions, because each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer in the group. If a topic has one partition, only one consumer can process it no matter how many you start. If it has twelve partitions, up to twelve consumers can process in parallel, giving roughly twelve times the throughput.

In Spring you simply run multiple instances of a service with the same group id, and Kafka distributes the partitions among them.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class TransactionProcessor {

    // Deploy 4 instances with same group id -> each gets 3 of 12 partitions
    @KafkaListener(
        topics = "transactions",
        groupId = "settlement-group",
        concurrency = "3"  // 3 threads per instance
    )
    public void processTransaction(String event) {
        System.out.println("Processing: " + event
            + " on thread " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (4 instances, 3 threads each = 12 consumers):
Instance A, Thread 0: processes partitions 0, 1, 2
Instance A, Thread 1: processes partitions 3, 4, 5
... (but Kafka assigns at most 12 consumers for 12 partitions)

With only 1 partition:
Instance A, Thread 0: processes partition 0
Instance A, Thread 1: IDLE (no partition available)
Instance B: IDLE (no partition available)
```

### How do partitions enable scalability?

Partitions can be spread across many brokers, so a single topic's data and load are not confined to one machine. Adding brokers and increasing partitions lets a topic scale horizontally to handle millions of events per second. Storage also scales because each broker holds only a subset of the partitions.

```java
package com.bank.scaling;

public class PartitionScaling {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Partition Distribution Across Brokers:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("3-broker cluster, 12 partitions:");
        System.out.println("  Broker 1: P0, P1, P2, P3");
        System.out.println("  Broker 2: P4, P5, P6, P7");
        System.out.println("  Broker 3: P8, P9, P10, P11");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("6-broker cluster, 12 partitions:");
        System.out.println("  Broker 1: P0, P1");
        System.out.println("  Broker 2: P2, P3");
        System.out.println("  Broker 3: P4, P5");
        System.out.println("  Broker 4: P6, P7");
        System.out.println("  Broker 5: P8, P9");
        System.out.println("  Broker 6: P10, P11");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("More brokers -> each holds less data and load");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Partition Distribution Across Brokers:

3-broker cluster, 12 partitions:
  Broker 1: P0, P1, P2, P3
  Broker 2: P4, P5, P6, P7
  Broker 3: P8, P9, P10, P11

6-broker cluster, 12 partitions:
  Broker 1: P0, P1
  Broker 2: P2, P3
  ...
More brokers -> each holds less data and load
```

### How do partitions control ordering?

Kafka guarantees ordering within a partition but not across partitions. This is actually a feature, because strict global ordering would force everything through a single partition and destroy parallelism. Instead you pick a key that defines your ordering requirement. In banking, keying by account id means all events for one account are ordered, which is usually exactly what you need.

```java
package com.bank.ordering;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class OrderedTransferProducer {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public OrderedTransferProducer(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void publishTransfer(String accountId, String type,
                                BigDecimal amount) {
        String event = "{\"accountId\":\"" + accountId
            + "\",\"type\":\"" + type
            + "\",\"amount\":" + amount + "}";
        // Key = accountId: all events for SAV2001 go to same partition
        // Rajesh's debit ALWAYS comes before his balance check
        kafkaTemplate.send("transactions", accountId, event);
        System.out.println(type + " for " + accountId + " -> same partition");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Demonstration of ordering guarantee
        System.out.println("SAV2001: DEBIT  -> partition 2, offset 0");
        System.out.println("SAV2001: CHECK  -> partition 2, offset 1");
        System.out.println("SAV2001: CREDIT -> partition 2, offset 2");
        System.out.println("Consumer reads: DEBIT, CHECK, CREDIT (in order)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("SAV3002: DEBIT  -> partition 5, offset 0");
        System.out.println("SAV3002 and SAV2001 process IN PARALLEL");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
SAV2001: DEBIT  -> partition 2, offset 0
SAV2001: CHECK  -> partition 2, offset 1
SAV2001: CREDIT -> partition 2, offset 2
Consumer reads: DEBIT, CHECK, CREDIT (in order)

SAV3002: DEBIT  -> partition 5, offset 0
SAV3002 and SAV2001 process IN PARALLEL
```

### What is partition skew and how do you avoid it?

Partition skew happens when a poorly chosen key sends disproportionate traffic to one partition. For example, if you key by transaction type and 90% of transactions are "TRANSFER", then 90% of events land in one partition while others sit idle. The solution is choosing a key with high cardinality that distributes evenly, like account id.

```java
package com.bank.skew;

public class PartitionSkewExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("BAD KEY: transaction type");
        System.out.println("  key='TRANSFER' -> 90% of events -> partition 3 (HOT)");
        System.out.println("  key='WITHDRAW' ->  5% of events -> partition 1 (idle)");
        System.out.println("  key='DEPOSIT'  ->  5% of events -> partition 0 (idle)");
        System.out.println("  Result: one consumer overloaded, others idle");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("GOOD KEY: account id");
        System.out.println("  key='SAV2001' -> partition 2 (balanced)");
        System.out.println("  key='SAV3002' -> partition 5 (balanced)");
        System.out.println("  key='SAV4003' -> partition 0 (balanced)");
        System.out.println("  Result: high cardinality, even distribution");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
BAD KEY: transaction type
  key='TRANSFER' -> 90% of events -> partition 3 (HOT)
  key='WITHDRAW' ->  5% of events -> partition 1 (idle)
  key='DEPOSIT'  ->  5% of events -> partition 0 (idle)
  Result: one consumer overloaded, others idle

GOOD KEY: account id
  key='SAV2001' -> partition 2 (balanced)
  key='SAV3002' -> partition 5 (balanced)
  key='SAV4003' -> partition 0 (balanced)
  Result: high cardinality, even distribution
```

### What happens if you have more consumers than partitions?

If there are more consumers in a group than partitions, the extra consumers sit idle because a partition can be owned by only one consumer at a time. They act as hot standbys, ready to take over if an active consumer fails.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

public class ConsumerVsPartitionCount {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Topic: transactions (3 partitions)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("3 consumers in group:");
        System.out.println("  Consumer A -> Partition 0 (active)");
        System.out.println("  Consumer B -> Partition 1 (active)");
        System.out.println("  Consumer C -> Partition 2 (active)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("5 consumers in group:");
        System.out.println("  Consumer A -> Partition 0 (active)");
        System.out.println("  Consumer B -> Partition 1 (active)");
        System.out.println("  Consumer C -> Partition 2 (active)");
        System.out.println("  Consumer D -> (IDLE, no partition)");
        System.out.println("  Consumer E -> (IDLE, no partition)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("If Consumer A dies, Consumer D takes Partition 0.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Topic: transactions (3 partitions)

3 consumers in group:
  Consumer A -> Partition 0 (active)
  Consumer B -> Partition 1 (active)
  Consumer C -> Partition 2 (active)

5 consumers in group:
  Consumer A -> Partition 0 (active)
  Consumer B -> Partition 1 (active)
  Consumer C -> Partition 2 (active)
  Consumer D -> (IDLE, no partition)
  Consumer E -> (IDLE, no partition)

If Consumer A dies, Consumer D takes Partition 0.
```

### What are common mistakes with partition planning?

The most common mistake is choosing too few partitions, which caps parallelism and makes it impossible to scale consumers later. Another mistake is increasing partitions after data is already flowing, which changes key-to-partition mapping and breaks per-key ordering for existing keys.

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// WRONG: Starting with 1 partition for a high-volume topic.
// You can never have more than 1 consumer, and increasing
// partitions later breaks key ordering.
public class WrongPartitionChoice {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: transactions topic with 1 partition");
        System.out.println("  Max consumers: 1 (no parallelism)");
        System.out.println("  Throughput: limited to one consumer");
        System.out.println("  Increasing to 6 later: changes hash mapping");
        System.out.println("  SAV2001 was on partition 0, now on partition 4");
        System.out.println("  Ordering broken for existing keys!");
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// CORRECT: Plan partitions with headroom from the start.
public class CorrectPartitionChoice {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("CORRECT: transactions topic with 12 partitions");
        System.out.println("  Current consumers: 4 (3 partitions each)");
        System.out.println("  Peak consumers: up to 12 (1 partition each)");
        System.out.println("  Headroom for scaling without repartitioning");
        System.out.println("  Key ordering preserved throughout lifecycle");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: 1 partition = no parallelism, repartition breaks ordering
CORRECT: 12 partitions = room to scale, ordering preserved
```

### What should a senior engineer know about partitions?

A senior engineer understands that choosing the partition count and the key is a real design decision, not a default. Too few partitions limits throughput. Too many partitions increase metadata overhead, leader election time, and open file handles on brokers. A good rule of thumb is to start with 2-3 times the expected peak consumer count, so you have room to scale without repartitioning.

```java
package com.bank.senior;

public class PartitionSizingGuide {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Partition Sizing Guide:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Formula: partitions = 2-3x expected peak consumers");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("National Bank of India examples:");
        System.out.println("  transactions: 12 partitions (peak 4 consumers)");
        System.out.println("  audit-events: 6 partitions (peak 2 consumers)");
        System.out.println("  trade-events: 24 partitions (peak 8 consumers)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Costs of too many partitions:");
        System.out.println("  - More open file handles per broker");
        System.out.println("  - Slower leader elections");
        System.out.println("  - More metadata in controller");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Costs of too few partitions:");
        System.out.println("  - Cannot scale consumer group");
        System.out.println("  - Repartitioning breaks key ordering");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Partition Sizing Guide:

Formula: partitions = 2-3x expected peak consumers

National Bank of India examples:
  transactions: 12 partitions (peak 4 consumers)
  audit-events: 6 partitions (peak 2 consumers)
  trade-events: 24 partitions (peak 8 consumers)

Costs of too many partitions:
  - More open file handles per broker
  - Slower leader elections
  - More metadata in controller

Costs of too few partitions:
  - Cannot scale consumer group
  - Repartitioning breaks key ordering
```
